        The article discusses the problem of baseline grid
typesetting with plain \TeX. It argues that not all structures
of \TeX\ should be forced to obey a grid. Macros are presented that
allow an author to type straight text on a grid if various
grid-leaving primitives and macros are avoided. Macros for
mathematics, boxes, and insertions are also developed; their
application sometimes requires manual assistance from the author.
